  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of a neutron scattering instrument, showing how a beam travels from generation to detection. A red beam leaves the source and passes through several beam-conditioning elements, including a chopper, a selector, and a monitor, which appear to shape, time, and track the incoming beam before it reaches the sample area.\n\nThe experiment takes place inside a large curved shielded enclosure. Near the center, the beam is further controlled by a PST chopper and a Doppler monochromator and drive, which suggests energy selection or modulation just before the beam hits the sample position. The sample sits above a cylindrical detector arrangement, while an analyser array curves around it. Red arrows indicate the beam striking the sample and then being scattered toward the analyser and detector system.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes the full measurement pathway: preparing the incident beam, delivering it to the sample, and collecting scattered particles with surrounding analyser and detector arrays. The circular geometry and heavy shielding highlight that the setup is designed for sensitive measurements of scattered neutrons, with multiple components arranged to control beam energy, timing, and detection around the sample.",
